e-asset-classification walkthrough log — 2026-10-10
Worked-case lesson: no command line by design (brief §6).
The "run" is the Aster Diagnostics classification scheme authored and self-reviewed in-session.

Scope: fictional Aster Diagnostics (240 people, clinical lab + SaaS patient portal;
LIMS, portal, data warehouse, email, file shares, laptops). All company facts fictional.
Artifacts authored:
  - Information-type inventory: 10+ types incl. containers (backups, logs, credentials)
  - Per-objective impact ratings (confidentiality/integrity/availability, low/mod/high
    per FIPS 199) with one-sentence reasons per rating
  - Four tiers with impact-anchored definitions + Aster-specific examples:
      Public / Internal / Confidential / Restricted
  - One named owner (role) per information type; dual-ownership dispute resolved
      (patient portal data split: CMO owns clinical content, CTO owns platform)
  - Handling hooks per tier (full matrix deferred to e-handling-requirements)
Design decisions recorded in lesson §3:
  1. Patient data = Confidential (not Restricted) to keep the top tier enforceable
  2. De-identified research data = Restricted (re-identification via aggregation)
  3. Integrity rated per type as notes, not a second tier system (FIPS-199 thinking,
     compressed for usability)
  4. One owner per type; auditor's "who decided?" must have one answer
  5. Scheme not approved until handling matrix existed (unhooked scheme = decoration)

